  "slug": "automated-microscopy-2e31",
  "summary": "Public tumour atlases and spatial resources are scaling up, with new tools linking cell annotation to spatial context.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the consolidation of tumour transcriptomic data into queryable atlases, with spatial context increasingly built into the analysis layer. TISCH3 expands to 477 datasets, including 69 spatial transcriptomics collections, while CROST 2.0 extends its public spatial repository to 544 projects and 3538 samples with niche, colocalization and cancer-niche tools. PANGEA remaps 12.6M cells into a 32-type, 165-subtype hierarchy spanning 160 diseases, and a pan-cancer screen of 42 cancers reports conserved tumour microenvironment programs. On the method side, SpaceMACE extends MACE to spatial transcriptomics, combining cell-type classification, CNV inference and spatial information for tumour-cell annotation. The lung cancer RNA-seq study of 1,824 tumours links nine transcriptional clusters to histology, genetics, immunity and survival. The items are not uniformly spatial; bulk and single-cell resources also feature.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
